- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **FX margin**: buy in INR, sell in USD; time the conversion, compare the AD bank rate with the escrow provider's rate, and add a small FX buffer to supplier offers. Optional forward cover for large deals.

### 11.2 Cut costs on every shipment — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Freight quotes from more than one source** (Maersk + forwarders) and book the cheapest that meets the date; today Maersk is booked by default.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Container fill**: maximise MT per container (20 ft vs 40 ft, bag weight) in the cost sheet and the offer quantity.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Inspection cost**: compare inspector quotes per booking; offer the buyer a cheaper inspection plan when it is enough.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Escrow provider by cost**: pick the cheaper provider per deal size when the buyer has no preference; pass the escrow fee to the buyer as a line item where allowed.

### 11.3 Price better — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Margin by corridor and buyer**: learn from accepted / countered indicative offers (outcomes already recorded) to set the target margin per HS code × market × buyer tier instead of one floor.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Value-added services priced explicitly**: private label packing, fumigation, palletising, extra inspections — a markup per service, shown in the offer.

### 11.4 Win more and repeat — New features to raise profit per deal (product)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **Repeat orders and annual contracts**: a "reorder" button, reminders before the buyer's next usual order date, framework contracts with monthly lifts.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Already-traded buyers and suppliers first**: a company PortLess has completed a deal with (PAID, no dispute or refund) is tried before a new one of the same product. Mark it on the directory record ("traded": deals completed, last deal, HS codes, dispute / refund count) and show it on Buyers & Suppliers and the radar. Use it in four places: (1) the scan adds traded companies for the HS code to the opportunity's buyers / suppliers even when the web search did not find them; (2) the outreach funnel contacts traded buyers first and the RFQ shortlist (`RFQ_MAX_SUPPLIERS`) always includes traded suppliers; (3) pair scoring and the match board rank a traded pair higher (today `outcomes.py` only adjusts the chance of closing); (4) before a negotiation opens with a new company, check whether a traded one can take the order (product, quantity, market, still passes validation) and ask it first. Never overrides a failed validation, a compliance hold or the margin floor; a past dispute or refund lowers the priority instead of raising it.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Buyer scoring for outreach**: focus the funnel on buyers most likely to close (reply rate, import history, fit score) — less outreach cost per deal.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Funnel analytics: scans → prospects → interested → matched → PI → paid, with time and cost per step.

### 12 Buyer and supplier validation (Opportunity Radar)
- [ ] **(Biz/Eng)** **Proof that a buyer actually imports this product.** Today a verified buyer is a real company with a working mailbox and a clean sanctions result, not a proven importer of the HS code. Needs import records (bill-of-lading data): paid Volza / ImportGenius, an Apify plan that can run the Store importer actors, or a new PortLess actor on public US customs bill-of-lading data (US-only coverage). See the decision below.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Outreach and RFQ only to verified counterparties (switch, e.g. `OUTREACH_VERIFIED_ONLY` / `RFQ_VERIFIED_ONLY`): today a failed buyer / supplier is never contacted, but an unverified one still is. Decide first (decisions).
- [x] **(Eng)** Sanctions "review" queue: counterparties whose screening returned `review` (possible match or a list unavailable) listed for staff, cleared or failed with a reason, audited like the compliance hold clearances; today they simply stay "unverified". — done 2026-11-18: `GET /trade-min/screening/review` (staff, oldest first) and `POST /trade-min/{buyers|suppliers}/{id}/screening-review` (clear / fail + reason) — the decision and what the screening said are kept on the record (`screening_reviews`); clear → verified when the e-mail is good, fail → failed (never contacted). Console: the queue card on Buyers & Suppliers.
- [x] **(Eng)** Re-check schedule: sanctions lists change — re-screen verified buyers / suppliers that are linked to open prospects or deals when their result passes `TRADE_MIN_VALIDATION_MAX_AGE_DAYS`, not only when a new scan finds them again. — done 2026-11-18: `services/trade_min/rescreen.py`, daily at 03:30 UTC (`scheduler_rescreen_cron`) and `POST /trade-min/screening/rescreen` (staff): verified companies of open prospects / deals whose check is older than the max age, oldest first, at most `TRADE_MIN_RESCREEN_MAX_PER_RUN` (20); now failed → `deal.exception` `SCREENING:rescreen_failed` on each open deal; review → the queue; no Apify token or no answer → the old result is kept.
- [x] **(Eng)** Confidence score counts the check: today `confidence_score` ignores validation (a corridor with 0 verified counterparties can score the same as one with 5). Add points for verified buyers / suppliers and show it in the radar's Confidence tooltip. — done 2026-11-18: up to `confidence_verified_max` (10) points — half for at least one verified buyer, half for at least one verified supplier (capped at 100); `confidence_basis` lists every part of the score and the radar shows it as the Confidence tooltip.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Pair matching in the "All" view: mark or down-rank a pair with an unverified side; the Best Match column in "All" can currently name an unverified pair.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Foreign buyer company existence beyond the website: LEI (GLEIF, already returned by the screening actor), EU VIES for EU buyers (returned), and a company-register / domain-age check for other countries; show them on the badge.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Console: show the supplier's `MatchResult.verification` + reasons on the prospect / deal page, and `BuyerCandidate.validation_status` in the outreach funnel list (backend fields exist, the deal pages do not show them yet).
- [ ] **(Ops)** Directory hygiene with the check: run "Check now" on worldwide-search companies before they are used (they are never checked automatically — cost), and a bulk "check all unverified" action with a cost estimate first.
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation: radar verified view hides unverified / failed, failed buyer never gets outreach, failed supplier never becomes the supplier party, reuse of a fresh result (no second actor run), no Apify token → nothing verified; browser case for the "Verified only / All" toggle and the directory filter.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Cost: show the validation actor runs per scan in the digest / cost dashboard (`APIFY_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY` budget is shared with discovery — a large scan can starve the check).

### 13.1 Keep a qualifying opportunity open — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** Switch `DEAL_COMPOSITION_ENABLED` (off by default until 13.4 is done): a prospect whose best pair scores at or above the threshold (`COMPOSITION_MIN_SCORE`, default = `MATCH_MIN_PROBABILITY`) is never dropped while one qualifying buyer and one qualifying supplier are still in play. A buyer declining, a supplier declining or one funnel running out no longer ends it; `deal_watcher` does not count a prospect with live offers as a stall.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Stop fixing the pair before negotiation. Today `matchmaking.confirm_match` sets one buyer + one supplier and `_withdraw_other_proposals` closes every other pair; the outreach funnel turns the first interested buyer into the buyer party (`outreach.py`, status `selected`) and stops contacting others; the RFQ winner becomes the supplier party. With composition on, every interested verified buyer and every quoting verified supplier stays a **candidate in negotiation**; buyer / supplier parties are set only by the composition decision (13.3).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Outreach keeps going after the first "interested" reply, up to `COMPOSITION_MAX_BUYERS` per prospect, so there is more than one buyer to compose for.
- [ ] **(Eng)** RFQ asks more than the top 3 when the buyers' total quantity is above the shortlisted suppliers' stated capacity (`RFQ_MAX_SUPPLIERS` is the cap).

### 13.2 Negotiate with every candidate on its own terms — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** One negotiation thread per counterparty. Today a `NegotiationSession` holds exactly one buyer and one supplier (`agents/negotiation_agent/schemas.py`). Each buyer thread negotiates the buyer's full requirement (`BuyerRequirement`: grade, quantity, incoterm, destination port, target price, delivery window, packing, inspection plan, escrow provider). Each supplier thread negotiates the supplier's full ability (`SupplierOffer`: grade, FOB / EXW price, MOQ, monthly capacity, port of loading, goods-ready date, packing, facility address).
- [ ] **(Eng)** A negotiation ends at a **firm offer**, not an agreement. Buyer: "Q MT of grade G at P, CIF port X, shipment by D, valid until V". Supplier: "up to Q MT of grade G at P FOB port Y, ready by D, valid until V". Store each one as a `FirmOffer` record with its validity. The e-mails say "subject to final order confirmation". Until composition there is no `CONFIRM_NEGOTIATION_AGREEMENT`, no PI and no firm order request.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Pricing per thread (`pricing.py`): the buyer band comes from the best supply mix currently available and the supplier ceiling from the best buyer price currently available. Today each comes from the one counterpart of the session. The margin floor is checked on the combination (13.3) as well as on each leg.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Partial quantity is not a reason to drop a supplier. When a supplier's capacity or offer is below what the buyers need, ask it for its maximum quantity and ready date, keep it, and ask the other suppliers for the rest of the quantity at the same grade.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Ask for a firm offer's validity and refresh it before it expires (one reminder, then mark it `expired`). An expired offer never enters a composition without a fresh price.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Privacy holds with N parties per prospect: a buyer never learns how many suppliers there are or who they are, and a supplier never learns about the buyers or the other suppliers. Extend `email_safety.preflight`, the draft leak checks and `knowledge_filter` from "the other party" to "every other party of the prospect".

### 13.3 Deal composition agent — choose the best deal after negotiation — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** New `shared/orchestration/composition.py`. Code makes the decision; the LLM only explains it, and no money decision depends on the LLM. Input: every live `FirmOffer` of the prospect, plus supplier offers of other prospects with the same HS code that are still valid. Output: proposed deals of one buyer × 1..N suppliers. Example: the buyer needs 1000 MT, supplier A offers 800 MT and supplier B 200 MT, so one deal is made with one buyer and two supplier legs. The objective is the highest expected margin after landed cost (each supplier's inland transport, port, freight plan from 13.5, one inspection per facility, escrow fee).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Hard rules for every combination:
  - compatible grade / spec;
  - every supplier's MOQ respected;
  - total within `COMPOSITION_QTY_TOLERANCE_PCT` of the buyer's quantity;
  - every leg ready before the buyer's shipment date;
  - ports of loading the same or consolidatable (13.5);
  - every party verified and clear of compliance holds, including related-party checks between the suppliers;
  - at most `COMPOSITION_MAX_SUPPLIERS` legs (start at 2–3);
  - margin floor on the total and on each leg.
- [ ] **(Eng)** No decision the moment a negotiation ends. Re-run the composition when an offer arrives, changes, is withdrawn or nears expiry, and when the buyer's deadline gets close. The agent answers **close now** (before the earliest validity in the best combination expires) or **wait** (more quotes pending that could beat it, with the expected gain). Always show the best single-supplier deal next to the composed one.
- [ ] **(Eng)** New gate `CONFIRM_DEAL_COMPOSITION` (L0, money-related: never auto-approved by a value threshold) showing the combination, the per-leg quantity / price / port / ready date, the margin per leg and in total, the freight plan, the risks and the runner-up combinations. APPROVE sets the buyer party and the supplier legs, sends each supplier a firm order request (`supplier_commitment` per leg), then confirms the buyer's agreement and issues the PI at the combined quantity.
- [ ] **(Eng)** A leg that declines or stays silent after APPROVE triggers a new composition from the remaining offers for that leg's quantity only. This generalises today's single standby supplier. If no valid combination is left, raise `deal.exception` `COMPOSITION:no_cover`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Candidates that were not chosen stay in the pool with their offers for the next composition or a second deal. They get no "closed" e-mail while their offer is valid. `outcomes.py` records the predicted vs realised margin of every composition for calibration.
- [ ] **(Eng)** `GET /deals/{id}/composition` (live offers, candidate combinations, score breakdown, why one won, what would change the answer) and `POST /deals/{id}/composition/run` (staff).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Console (portless-ui): a composition card on the prospect page with offers per side, the proposed combination, alternatives and validity countdowns. The approval card shows the legs.

### 13.4 Running a deal with more than one supplier — Deal composition after negotiation
Everything after the match assumes one supplier party today: PO, QC, loading, payouts, retention, ledger, documents, compliance, notifications.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Data model: `DealRecord.supplier_legs` (supplier, quantity, price, basis, port of loading, ready date, facility address, PO, QC orders, status). Money, QC and documents read the legs instead of the single supplier party. A migration turns every existing deal into one leg; one-supplier deals behave exactly as today.
- [ ] **(Eng)** One PO per leg, with the margin guard on each leg and on the total. PO acknowledgement, goods-ready date and facility address are tracked per leg, and the watcher sends reminders per leg.
- [ ] **(Eng)** QC per facility: one QIMA order per leg, loading supervision and seals per leg. The deal's `qc_status` passes only when every leg passed. A failed leg is replaced (13.3 recomposition) or that leg's quantity is cancelled with the buyer's consent; one leg does not take the whole deal into `DISPUTE`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Money: the buyer still has one escrow. Payouts, supplier retention, `available_for_payout`, the ledger and the journal are kept per leg (released tranches split pro rata by leg value). The deal is `PAID` only when every leg is paid in full. Four-eyes and the compliance money block apply per payout.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Documents. The buyer side is unchanged: one PI / Sales Contract / CI / PL at the combined quantity, and no supplier is named. The CI / PL list the containers and seals of every leg. On the supplier side, each PO and notice names only that supplier's own leg. Whether the SDF / Shipping Bill is one or one per port of loading follows the CHA decision.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Compliance: trade check, screening and related-party check for every leg. The shipment check applies the quantity tolerance to the total loaded against the PI and to each leg against its PO.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Deal board / timeline / evidence pack: a status per leg plus the aggregate (e.g. "QC 1 of 2 passed"); the evidence pack has a section per leg.

### 13.5 Freight — fewer containers and cheaper boxes — Deal composition after negotiation
- [ ] **(Eng)** Container plan instead of a fixed 25 MT per box. Today both `trade_costs.py` (`round(qty / 25 + 0.49)`) and `MaerskConnector.build_booking` (`MAERSK_MT_PER_CONTAINER`, 25) assume 25 MT. Plan per product line: payload and volume limits by equipment (20 ft / 40 ft / 40 HC), bag size and density from the packing spec, and the remainder as a smaller box or LCL. Suggest rounding the offer quantity to full containers when that raises the margin. The cost sheet shows the plan.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Inside a multi-supplier deal, compare per leg and pick the cheapest option that meets the ready dates:
  - (a) each supplier stuffs its own containers at its own port;
  - (b) truck one leg to the other leg's port or a CFS and stuff together;
  - (c) send a small leg as LCL.

  The inspector supervises each stuffing point. The choice feeds the composition score (13.3).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Get freight quotes from the endpoints that exist: `CogoportConnector.quote` (already FCL / LCL; pass container type, cargo weight and LCL volume), `FreightosConnector.quote` and Maersk. `MaerskConnector.quote` today returns only the contract rate from `MAERSK_RATE_USD_PER_CONTAINER`; confirm the Maersk Product Offers / spot-rate API on the account and wire it. Compare quotes for each container plan and record the chosen quote with its source on the cost sheet. This overlaps 11.2 "freight quotes from more than one source".

### 13.6 Apify actors for quality trade and trade information — Deal composition after negotiation
Build each one with `/actor-pipeline` on the `rich_minds` account. That plan cannot run Store actors, so they must be PortLess's own actors. Each one needs a connector + normaliser in `shared/connectors/apify.py`, a readiness check, a place in the `APIFY_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY` budget, and pinned stub mode in tests.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Supplier quality / certification check**: FSSAI licence, APEDA RCMC, Spices Board / commodity-board registration, organic (NPOP), and ISO / BRC / HACCP certificates from public registries and the supplier's site. Feeds supplier trust, the composition's grade fit, and the documents the buyer asks for.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Rejection history**: EU RASFF notifications and US FDA import refusals by product and by Indian exporter. A risk flag per supplier and per HS × market; a recent refusal forces the composition gate to show it.
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation:
  - 1000 MT buyer covered by 800 + 200 MT suppliers, through to PAID;
  - supplier B declines after APPROVE, the composition re-runs for 200 MT;
  - an expired offer is excluded;
  - margin floor broken on the total although one leg clears it;
  - a buyer e-mail never reveals the number of suppliers;
  - one leg fails QC, only that leg is replaced;
  - payouts per leg and PAID only when both legs are paid.

  Plus an LLM journey UC-17: like UC-15, with one buyer and two suppliers.

### 14.1 Legal entity — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal)** After incorporation: first board meeting, statutory auditor appointed within 30 days (ADT-1), commencement of business declaration (INC-20A) within 180 days, share certificates issued.
- [ ] **(Biz/Legal)** Founders' / shareholders' agreement (roles, equity, vesting, who signs and who approves money).
- [ ] **(Legal)** Trademark application for "PortLess" (name and logo) in the relevant classes (trading / logistics services).

### 14.2 Tax and trade registrations — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal)** Udyam (MSME) registration — free; gives priority-sector lending, protection against late payment, and access to MSME export schemes.
- [ ] **(Legal)** FSSAI licence for food products — confirm with the CA / FSSAI which licence a merchant exporter of your products needs (central licence is typical for exporters).
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Plant-quarantine registration for agri exports so the phytosanitary certificate can be applied for (also 3.5).
- [ ] **(Legal)** State registrations: Shops & Establishments licence for the office, professional tax (employer and directors).
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Portals the business must use: DGFT, ICEGATE, GST, e-way bill (inland truck to port, also 3.5) and e-invoicing once turnover crosses the threshold.

### 14.3 Bank and payment accounts — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Pre-shipment / packing credit line with the bank (finances the supplier advance when a buyer's escrow is not yet released) and post-shipment credit if needed.
- [ ] **(Biz)** ECGC export credit insurance (policy and buyer limits) — cover against buyer default or country risk outside the escrow.
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Open marine cargo insurance policy with an insurer or broker (also 1.4 `PORTLESS_MARINE_POLICY`).
- [ ] **(Biz)** Accounting software (Tally / Zoho Books) from day one, chart of accounts agreed with the CA (also 4).
- [ ] **(Biz)** Forward-contract (FX hedging) facility with the bank for large USD receipts (also 11.1).

### 14.4 Brand, website and contact details — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Business phone and WhatsApp Business number (local suppliers often answer only there), shown on the website and in the signature (`PORTLESS_PHONE`).
- [ ] **(Biz)** LinkedIn company page and a Google Business Profile for the office — buyers check both before replying to a first e-mail.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Company profile / brochure PDF, letterhead, rubber stamp for signed export documents, visiting cards.
- [ ] **(Biz)** D-U-N-S number (Dun & Bradstreet, free) — many foreign buyers and their banks look a supplier up by it.
- [ ] **(Biz)** Fill the company profile settings once these exist: `PORTLESS_LEGAL_NAME`, `PORTLESS_WEBSITE`, `PORTLESS_CONTACT_EMAIL`, `PORTLESS_PHONE`, `PORTLESS_IEC`, `PORTLESS_GSTIN` (also 1.4).

### 14.5 Advisers and service providers — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Company secretary (ROC filings) and a trade lawyer for the contracts (also 3.6).
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Accounts with the providers the workflow uses: QIMA (inspection), Maersk (carrier) and one freight forwarder as a back-up (also 1.2, 2.3).
- [ ] **(Biz/Ops)** Courier account (DHL / FedEx / Aramex) for sending original documents (B/L, certificate of origin) to buyers' banks and samples to buyers.

### 14.6 Office, people and records — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Biz)** Name board at the registered office (GST verification may check it) and a filing system for original documents (export documents must be kept for years, also 3.7).
- [ ] **(Biz)** People for the roles the platform expects: trade desk operator, money approver (second person for four-eyes), compliance officer (also 4, 8).

### 14.7 Product and market requirements — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** Per destination: what the buyer's country needs before the goods arrive — e.g. US FDA food-facility registration and prior notice, Gulf halal certificate / SFDA (Saudi), EU pesticide residue limits (MRL) and certificate of analysis. Record it per product line in the catalogue.

### 14.8 Ongoing compliance calendar — Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)
- [ ] **(Legal/Ops)** One calendar with an owner for every recurring filing: monthly GST returns (GSTR-1, GSTR-3B), TDS returns, IEC annual update (April – June), LUT renewal (April), RCMC renewal, ROC annual filings (AOC-4, MGT-7), directors' KYC (DIR-3 KYC), income-tax return and tax audit, DSC renewal, insurance renewals.

### 15.1 What is stored — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
- [ ] **(Ops/Eng)** **Specification template per catalogue product**: each `ProductLine` lists the attributes that define a tradeable lot of that product, each with allowed values and a unit. Onion (HS 0703.10): form (fresh / dehydrated — a different HS code), variety and colour (Nashik red, pink, white, Bellary, Krishnapuram rose), bulb size in mm (45 to 55, 55+), crop (new / stored, month), quality tolerances (sprouting, rot, double bulbs, moisture), packing (5 / 10 / 25 / 50 kg mesh or jute bag), container (reefer or ventilated, MT per box). Without a template the product stays free text, as today.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Buyer requirement on the directory record, per product**: what this buyer wants of this product, not only that it "buys onion": the template attributes plus quantity per order, how often, destination port, incoterm, target price and its basis, certificates and documents asked for (phytosanitary, fumigation, origin, organic), shelf life on arrival, label / private label, payment terms, season it buys in.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Supplier offer on the directory record, per product**: what this supplier can actually supply: varieties, sizes and grades it packs, capacity per month, minimum order, months it has stock, packing options, price with its basis (ex-works / FOB) and date, certificates it holds (FSSAI, APEDA, GlobalG.A.P.), own packhouse or trader, plant location and nearest port.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Every fact carries its source and date**: company website, the buyer's / supplier's own e-mail, a completed deal, or staff. A fact nobody stated is shown as "not known", never filled from the HS code or a guess. A newer statement from the company replaces an older one; a staff entry is never overwritten by a scan.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Exact product code and name**: store the 8-digit ITC-HS code where known (the 6-digit code mixes different goods), one product name per catalogue line with its synonyms mapped to it (onion / shallot / "big onion"), and keep fresh, dried and processed forms as separate products.

### 15.2 How it is filled — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
- [ ] **(Eng)** **From the company's own website at scan time**: the contact finder already opens the site; read the product page into the template (varieties, sizes, packing, certificates) for the top N companies, with the page as the source.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **From replies, and kept**: an outreach reply is already read into `BuyerRequirement` and a quote into `SupplierOffer`, but both live on the prospect and are lost to the directory when it closes. Copy them onto the company's record, and extend both readers to the template attributes (today: one free-text `grade` and `packaging`).
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Ask for what is missing**: the buyer introduction and the RFQ list the product's unanswered template attributes as short questions (variety, size, packing, quantity per order, port), not a general "send your requirement"; the negotiation's `missing_information` uses the same list.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **From completed deals**: at PAID, write the PI / PO specification, quantity, packing and price onto both companies' records as their proven requirement / offer (feeds 11.4 "already-traded first").
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Staff edit**: a form on the company's page to enter or correct the requirement / offer per product (a phone call, a trade-fair meeting), audited.

### 15.3 How it is used — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Match on the specification, not the HS code**: a buyer of 55 mm+ red onion in 25 kg bags is paired only with a supplier that packs it; a conflict on variety, size, form or packing is a hard filter and each matching attribute raises the pair score. Today a web-search buyer's product fit is a fixed 85 and only `grade` conflicts are checked.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Buyers & Suppliers page**: one crisp line per product on each row ("Onion · red · 55 mm+ · 25 kg mesh · 2 × 40 ft reefer / month · CIF Jebel Ali"), how complete the requirement is (e.g. 6 of 9 known), a detail panel with each fact's source and date, and filters by product and attribute (variety, size, packing, port).
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Radar and prospect pages** show the same line for each buyer and supplier, and what is still unknown before a deal starts; the pre-deal rescore lists the missing attributes under "still needed".
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Carried into the deal**: the agreed specification fills the PI / PO description, the packing spec, the QC booking (inspection criteria, AQL, QIMA product ids) and the cost sheet (reefer vs dry box, MT per container) instead of being typed again at each step.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Export policy per exact product**: the trade check reads the 8-digit code and form; onion is a product India has restricted before (export ban, minimum export price), so the current DGFT notification is checked before outreach and again before the PI (3.4, compliance seed).
- [ ] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + automation: a requirement read from a reply lands on the directory record with its source; an unknown attribute is never invented; a variety / size / packing conflict blocks the pair; the outreach e-mail asks only the unanswered attributes; a staff entry survives the next scan; browser case for the product line, completeness and filters.

### 15.4 How much the buyer buys — before and after contact — Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier
**Today (2026-11-18).** *After contact* the quantity is known: the introduction asks "Quantity (MT) and required delivery date", the reply is read into `BuyerRequirement.quantity_mt` (LLM gateway, regex fallback for MT / tons / containers / kg), the negotiation asks again while it is missing (`briefing.buyer_missing`), staff can set it (`PUT /deals/{id}/buyer-candidates/{cid}/requirement`), and the deal takes it as `quantity_mt` (else the radar's lot). *Before contact* nothing is known: only the US-importers actor returns a buyer's yearly volume, and the Apify plan cannot run it, so every web-search buyer has no volume, the pair score's **volume fit** (12–15 % of the weight) is always blank and the radar sizes every buyer at one container.
- [ ] **(Eng) P1** **Volume from import records** — per buyer: shipments in the last 12 months, total MT, typical shipment size, how often, last shipment date, main origins. Source: public US bill-of-lading data (own actor, US buyers), paid Volza / ImportGenius / Panjiva for other markets — the decision in 12. Fills `BuyerSignal.annual_volume_mt` / `shipments_12m` / `last_import_date` (fields exist), so volume fit, reorder timing (`ranking.reorder_probability`, unused today) and yearly potential (16.3) work.
- [ ] **(Eng) P1** **Quantities stated in public demand** — tenders, buy requests and RFQ boards state a quantity (21.3 `food-tenders-ai`, `buy-leads-ai`); carry it onto the buyer as a *stated* quantity with its source and date.
- [ ] **(Eng) P2** **Order-size band when nothing is stated** — small / medium / large from company signals (employees, warehouses or branches on its site, number of product lines, the market's import value ÷ the number of importers found), shown as a band and labelled an estimate, never a quantity in an offer.
- [ ] **(Eng) P1** **Show and use it**: "Buys ~X MT/yr · ships every ~N weeks (source, date)" on the directory card and profile, the radar's buyer list and the prospect desk; the radar's lot and the deal's default quantity use a stated or recorded quantity before the one-container default; pair volume fit stops being blank.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Quantity read from a reply is kept and checked**: write it onto the directory record with its source (15.2 "from replies, and kept"); a reply in containers, bags or kg is converted with the product's container / bag size (catalogue, 16.3) and echoed back in the next e-mail for the buyer to confirm ("2 × 20' ≈ 36 MT — correct?").
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Quantity against supply**: when the buyer's quantity exceeds the chosen supplier's stated capacity or MOQ blocks it, the desk says so and points to a second supplier (13.4 composition) instead of the match board only listing a blocker.
- [ ] **(QA)** Tests: volume from a recorded import-record row reaches volume fit and the radar lot; a reply in containers is converted and confirmed; a band is never used as an offer quantity.

### 16.1 Hand-off from the radar to the prospect — Opportunity Radar: profit and next step
- [x] **(Eng)** **Ready-to-start checklist per opportunity** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/start_check.py`, `EnrichedOpportunity.readiness`): reachable buyers (usable e-mail, not flagged / failed; at least `OUTREACH_PARALLEL`), reachable suppliers, export policy, price checked, margin at the floor, duty verified, market news, deal already running, destination requirements — ok / warn / fail. The radar row shows one dot per check ("ready" / "N to check" / "N blocking"), the expanded row the list; "Start prospect" names every failing check and asks to confirm.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Carry the radar's numbers into the deal** — done 2026-11-17: the match seed passes `profit`, `lot_mt` and `flags`; the deal keeps them unchanged as `DealRecord.radar_estimate`, takes the radar's lot as its default `quantity_mt` and the radar's buyer price as its target when the scan had none (`buyer_price_source = radar:<basis>`, so the RFQ no longer falls back to "1.25 × cheapest FOB" when the radar had a price). Deal page: "Radar estimate → negotiated → realised" card.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Estimate vs outcome per opportunity** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/accuracy.py`, `GET /trade-min/profit/accuracy`): realised ÷ estimated margin per settled deal; with ≥ 3 settled deals of a product (else ≥ 5 overall) the radar multiplies its margin estimate by the median ratio (clamped 0.5–1.2) and shows "corrected: …". Learns as soon as deals settle — nothing to do until then.

### 16.2 What can stop a deal, shown before it starts — Opportunity Radar: profit and next step
- [x] **(Eng)** **Export policy on the radar** — done 2026-11-17: `compliance.check_hs()` + `check_destination()` at scan time (a prohibited product or embargoed market is dropped before any paid search, with the reason in the scan report) and on every list (never listed; "Export policy unconfirmed" flag, chance of closing unchanged, checklist amber). Staff confirm "free to export" from the expanded row (`PUT /deals/compliance/hs-policy/{hs}`, recorded with their e-mail).
- [ ] **(Ops/Legal)** Confirm the export policy of every catalogue HS code once against ITC-HS Schedule 2 / DGFT notifications (the radar's "Confirm free to export", or restricted / prohibited through the API) — today every code shows "unconfirmed" (all live opportunities). 8-digit codes and minimum export prices stay in 15.3.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Market news verdict on the radar** — done 2026-11-17: `market_check.run` at scan and rescore (cached `MARKET_CHECK_MAX_AGE_HOURS`), the list reads the cache only; avoid ×0.5 and caution ×0.85 on the chance of closing, "News: avoid / caution" flags, verdict + summary in the market section, checklist red / amber. Needs `TAVILY_API_KEY` for the best results (Google News RSS without it) and an LLM gateway for the verdict.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Destination import requirements on the row** — done 2026-11-17: catalogue `ProductLine.import_requirements` (per ISO-2 market or `*`), shown in the market section and as an amber check.
- [ ] **(Ops/Legal)** Fill `import_requirements` per catalogue product line (FDA registration / prior notice, halal / SFDA, EU MRL certificate, phytosanitary …) — the data half of 14.7.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Data year and staleness** — done 2026-11-17: `data_period` and `market_data_flags` kept from the export-market row; "Comtrade <year> · old" when older than two years, flag "Old prices", tip in "What's missing". Opportunities scanned before today show it after their next scan.

### 16.3 A truer profit number — Opportunity Radar: profit and next step
- [x] **(Biz/Eng)** **Export incentives as income** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/incentives.py`, Mongo `export_incentives`, `GET /trade-min/incentives`, `PUT /trade-min/incentives/{hs}` staff): RoDTEP % (+ cap) and drawback % (+ cap) per 8 / 6 / 4-digit code, counted as + USD/MT in the margin only when `eligible_confirmed`; profit panel line "Export incentives".
- [ ] **(Ops/CA)** Enter the RoDTEP (DGFT Appendix 4R) and drawback (CBIC schedule) rates for each catalogue HS code and tick eligibility once the CA confirms it for PortLess as a merchant exporter (11.1) — until then incentives are "not counted".
- [x] **(Eng)** **Freight per lane, not per region** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/freight_lanes.py`, Mongo `freight_lane_rates`, `GET/PUT /trade-min/freight-lanes`): a forwarder quote per lane (asked at scan time only when `COGOPORT_API_KEY` / `FREIGHTOS_API_KEY` is real — never a placeholder, stub quotes ignored) or a quote staff entered, used for `LANE_QUOTE_MAX_AGE_DAYS` (14), else the regional estimate; basis shown on the freight line.
- [x] **(Eng/Ops)** **Container fit per product** — done 2026-11-17 (engineering): catalogue `container_mt` and `container_type` (dry / reefer, reefer estimate × `REEFER_FREIGHT_FACTOR` 1.6) drive the margin per container, the default lot and the freight.
- [ ] **(Ops)** Fill `container_mt` / `container_type` per catalogue product and packing (fresh onion = reefer; ground spices 15–18 MT per 20') — part of area 10 "packing standards".
- [x] **(Eng)** **A range, not one number** — done 2026-11-17: expected profit low / high from the supplier price spread (supplier prices min–max, price book p25–p75, mandi min / max, ±15 % on India's export average), shown under the expected profit and in the panel.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Live USD/INR in the mandi price** — done 2026-11-17: the mandi connector converts at the live rate (`market_intel.fetch_usd_inr_rate`) and prints the rate and its source; fallback rate when the live one is unavailable.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Mandi trend and season** — done 2026-11-17 (`services/trade_min/mandi_history.py`, Mongo `mandi_price_history`): every mandi summary fetched is kept per commodity per day; after 7 days of history the panel says rising / falling / flat with "buy now" / "wait or ask for a fresh quote". Starts collecting once `DATA_GOV_IN_API_KEY` is set (owner track A).
- [ ] **(Eng)** Mandi history backfill from Agmarknet's historical datasets on data.gov.in so the trend works from day one, plus a harvest-month calendar per commodity for the seasonal factor.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Annual potential next to one lot** — done 2026-11-17 (market basis): "Profit per year" = margin × min(`reachable_share_pct` 1 % of the market's yearly import tonnes, one lot a month) × chance of closing; sort option "Profit per year".
- [ ] **(Eng)** Annual potential per buyer from its own reorder frequency once import records exist (decision in 12: bill-of-lading data), and from Comtrade monthly data (export-market actor `frequency: "M"`, more actor cost).
- [x] **(Eng)** **Scan cost against profit** — done 2026-11-17: an Apify cost meter (`apify.start_cost_meter`) per corridor scan and per rescore → `EnrichedOpportunity.scan_cost` (runs, USD from `usageTotalUsd`), shown in the market section.

### 16.4 Console — Opportunity Radar: profit and next step
- [x] **(Eng)** **"What's missing and where to get it"** — done 2026-11-17: `profit.missing` (no supplier price → `DATA_GOV_IN_API_KEY` / Rescore / RFQ; no buyer price; duty unverified → Access2Markets / MacMap; price check; freight estimate → quote keys or a staff quote; incentives not counted; export policy unconfirmed; old Comtrade year), listed under the profit panel.
- [x] **(Eng)** **Urgency in the table** — done 2026-11-17: "time-sensitive · T3/T4" chip with RFQs / tenders / disruption in its tooltip, sort "Time-sensitive".
- [x] **(Eng)** **Prospect page shows the opportunity it came from** — done 2026-11-17: the deal's Origin Opportunity card shows the radar's flags, profit breakdown and market section; Overview shows "Radar estimate → negotiated → realised".
- [x] **(QA)** Gherkin cases + browser cases — done 2026-11-17: backend `tests/test_radar_handoff.py` (27) and `tests/test_opportunity_profit.py` (40); browser cases TC-WEB-033 … TC-WEB-040 in `docs/testing/BROWSER_SCENARIOS.md` (not automated: no browser runner yet, 6.2).


### 17.1 National trade statistics next to Comtrade — Trade data beyond UN Comtrade
- [ ] **(Eng)** **UK HMRC trade data connector** (free API, uktradeinfo.com OData; monthly imports by CN8 code and partner, value **and kg**): fills the UK lines where Comtrade has a value but no quantity, so no unit price and no gap (14 UK buyers dropped as "no measured price gap" on 2026-11-17). Direct connector, no Apify cost; Phase 1 source for market `GB`.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Eurostat Comext connector** (free API; monthly EU imports by CN8 code, partner and quantity in 100 kg): fresher than Comtrade's annual figures and 8-digit (grade-level) codes for DE, ES, NL, FR, IT … Direct connector; Phase 1 source for EU markets.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **US Census** — already a connector: turn it on with the free `CENSUS_API_KEY` (owner track A) and give it the US monthly import price per HS10 as the buyer anchor when fresher than Comtrade.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **India's own export statistics — new PortLess Apify actor** (`india-export-stats-ai`, built with `/actor-pipeline`): Ministry of Commerce / DGCI&S TradeStat (tradestat.commerce.gov.in — web form, no API) gives India's exports by 8-digit ITC-HS code × destination × month, value and quantity. Use it for: (1) what each market pays for INDIA's goods this year (the radar's `india_to_market` anchor, today Comtrade, often two years old); (2) a supplier-side check (India's FOB per destination); (3) whether India already ships the product to the market at all; (4) the trend per destination. Cache a month (published monthly).
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Mirror data for markets that report late or not at all** (UAE, other Gulf states, many African markets): use partners' exports to the market (Comtrade mirror flows; the export-market actor already returns a `mirror` flag) and India's exports to it (actor above) when the market's own import figures are missing or older than two years. UAE data on 2026-11-17 is from 2023 and shows prices below India's average (negative gaps, 23 UAE buyers dropped).
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Investigate UAE / Dubai customs open data** (Dubai Pulse / Federal Competitiveness and Statistics Centre) as a direct source for the UAE; an Apify actor only if there is no API.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **One merge rule for every source**: per product × market take the freshest source with a quantity (HMRC / Comext / Census monthly → TradeStat → Comtrade annual → mirror), record which one on the opportunity (`price_benchmark_source`, `data_period`), and show the source and month on the radar. A source that disagrees by more than 30 % with another is flagged, not averaged.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **8-digit codes end to end**: the national sources speak CN8 / HS10 / ITC-HS8; map them to the catalogue's product lines (15.1 "exact product code") so a price is per grade, not per 6-digit mix — fixes many "price check needed" gaps.
- [ ] **(Eng)** Demand gate uses these sources too: a market with recent monthly imports from a national source is "live demand" even when Comtrade has nothing (89 buyers were dropped as "no live demand data" on 2026-11-17).
- [ ] **(QA)** Tests per connector on recorded responses (`httpx.MockTransport`), the merge rule (freshest-with-quantity wins, disagreement flagged), and the actor's normaliser; readiness shows each source live / stub.


### 18.1 Opportunities from companies, not only from market statistics — How opportunities are found
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Company-led opportunities**: group the buyer / supplier directory by product × country; where at least `COMPANY_LED_MIN_BUYERS` (3) checked buyers with a usable e-mail and at least 2 reachable suppliers exist, create an opportunity even without a Comtrade gap. Price it from the price book / mandi / any market price, mark it "no market statistics" (`source=directory`), and let the profit ranking and the ready-to-start checklist judge it like any other. On 2026-11-17 the UAE had 42 named importers and no opportunity; 232 of 287 directory buyers were linked to none.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Profit gate instead of the gap gate**: today a corridor without a measured Comtrade gap builds no opportunity (`_scan_one`: "no live demand data" / "no measured price gap" dropped 126 buyers on 2026-11-17). Keep a corridor whenever buyer price (any source) − supplier price (any source) − landed costs clears the margin floor; the Comtrade gap becomes one input and a warning (`price_check_needed`, `no_market_statistics`), never the gate. Never invent a price: no price on one side still means no opportunity, listed in the scan report with what is missing.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **The worldwide search adds markets**: a country where the worldwide buyer search (`_worldwide_counterparties`, 53 companies on 2026-11-17, "not tied to a scanned market") found at least N buyers of a product is proposed as a new market for that catalogue line (staff accept with one click on the Catalogue page) and scanned next time, instead of the companies staying in the directory only.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Demand signals create opportunities**: a buyer's inbound reply or enquiry (e-mail mapped to no deal), an RFQ or tender, a buyer who asked for another product during outreach, and a past buyer due to reorder (11.4 "repeat orders") each create or refresh an opportunity for that product × market with the buyer attached first — the strongest demand there is, today none of them can start one.
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Supplier-led opportunities**: a checked Indian supplier with a price and capacity for a product (scan, RFQ reply, manual supplier 5.5) is matched against every market whose directory buyers want the product, not only the scanned corridors; surplus capacity offered by a supplier starts a search for buyers (links 5.5 "supply-first deal").
- [ ] **(Eng/Ops)** **Clean the roles in the directory**: run the quality check (`quality.py`) over the stored directory — "buyers" in India for export markets and companies flagged `role_suspect` become suppliers (when they make the product) or are dropped from outreach; today the check only runs on new scans (30 Indian "buyers" on 2026-11-17).
- [ ] **(Eng)** **Radar shows how each opportunity was found**: source chip "market statistics" / "directory" / "buyer enquiry" / "supplier offer" / "worldwide search", filter by it, and count per source in the scan report — so the operator can compare which source turns into deals (feeds 11.4 funnel analytics).
- [ ] **(QA)** Tests: company-led opportunity from 3 checked buyers + 2 suppliers without Comtrade; profit gate keeps a corridor with no gap but a clearing margin and drops one with a price missing (scan report says what); a proposed market from the worldwide search; an inbound enquiry creates an opportunity; Indian "buyer" re-roled; browser case for the source chip and filter.


### 19.1 Prospect desk — trading the prospect stage
- [x] **(Eng)** **Prospect desk** — done 2026-11-17 (`shared/orchestration/prospect_desk.py`, `GET /deals/prospects/desk`, Prospects page): per prospect the best buyer × supplier pair (margin/MT, chance, expected value, firm or estimate), the radar's original estimate, whose move it is (`us` / `buyers` / `buyer` / `suppliers`) and for how many days, stalled (idle ≥ `PROSPECT_STALL_DAYS` 5), quotes expiring / expired, undelivered introductions, and ranked next moves (confirm, send offer, push supplier, best and final, reconfirm quote, contact buyers, chase quotes, fix mailbox, park after `PROSPECT_DROP_DAYS` 21, drop). Summary cards: pipeline value, ready to confirm, our move, stalled, quotes to reconfirm; sort by priority / value / idle / newest; filter "Our move" / "Stalled".
- [x] **(Eng)** **Walk-away and target prices per pair** — done 2026-11-17 (`matchmaking.negotiation_targets`): highest supplier FOB and lowest buyer price that keep the margin floor, and both at the target margin (`MATCH_TARGET_MARGIN_PCT` 12); "push the supplier to ≤ X FOB (−Y/MT) or the buyer to ≥ Z" as the next step when under the floor; match board column "Walk-away".
- [x] **(Eng)** **Quotes: age, validity, leverage** — done 2026-11-17: quote age and days of validity left per pair, "expires in N days — ask to reconfirm" within 3 days, expired quotes listed on the board (still not scored), best quote and spread with "ask the other suppliers to match or beat it".
- [x] **(Eng)** **One cost model for radar and match board** — done 2026-11-17: the board uses the radar's lane freight (`freight_lanes`), reefer per product and confirmed export incentives (`incentives`), shown in its assumptions.
