## Appendix — background per area

### 1 Accounts, credentials and configuration (readiness gate)

Each item is a failing or warning readiness check today.

### 2 Provider contracts not yet confirmed

Each provider was built from public documentation; these points were not confirmed by the provider and must be checked against a real account before money moves.

### 3 Legal, regulatory and trade compliance (India export)

PortLess is the exporter of record, buys from Indian suppliers and sells to foreign buyers. Every point needs a lawyer / chartered accountant's sign-off; the seed lists in the code are not the law.

### 5.5 Add suppliers manually (local suppliers)

The trade desk knows local suppliers that no scan will find. Today they cannot be added so that
the workflow takes over:
- the supplier directory (`trade_counterparties`) is written only by a scan — there is no add / edit endpoint and no console screen (buyers can be added to a deal, suppliers cannot);
- a deal created by hand with a supplier party but no scan opportunity gets **no RFQ**: the RFQ leg finds no candidates (`_rfq_candidates` reads only Match Engine results or the opportunity) and leaves the deal parked in MATCHING;
- `POST /deals/{id}/supplier-offers` (an offer entered by staff) runs neither the GST / IEC verification nor the match board — someone has to call `/match/evaluate` by hand.
Backend
Console (portless-ui)
QA

### 11 New features to raise profit per deal (product)

Ordered by expected impact on margin. Each should be measured on closed deals (realised margin vs cost sheet).

### 12 Buyer and supplier validation (Opportunity Radar)

**Where the check belongs: the radar first, the deal again.** A company that is sanctioned, fake or
unreachable should never be shown as an opportunity's buyer or supplier, never get an outreach
e-mail and never become a deal party, so the first check runs in the scan, before the opportunity
is saved, on the companies the radar would show (top N per role, results reused for 30 days to keep
Apify cost down). The deal keeps its own checks because facts change between the scan and the
money: the buyer is screened again before the PI, the supplier's GST / IEC are verified at quote
intake, and trade compliance runs at deal creation and before the PI. Checking only on the deal
board (the old design) meant the radar listed unchecked companies and the outreach funnel could
pitch one before anyone looked.

Verdict rules: `verified` only on positive answers (sanctions clear + deliverable or catch-all
e-mail + no registry red flag); `failed` only on negative answers (sanctions block, GST / IEC
inactive, DGFT denied); everything else `unverified`. Without an Apify token nothing is verified.

### 13 Deal composition after negotiation (more buyers, more suppliers, cheaper freight)

**The idea.** A scan gives many buyer and supplier candidates per opportunity. Today PortLess picks
one buyer and one supplier *before* negotiating: the match board's CONFIRM_MATCH or the first
interested buyer plus the RFQ winner. After that, every other candidate is closed, and the deal
fails if that one pair fails. The new flow keeps a good opportunity open. It negotiates with every
qualifying buyer on the buyer's full requirement and with every qualifying supplier on the
supplier's full ability, collects firm offers with a validity date, and only then lets a
composition agent decide the most profitable deal from what was actually offered.

**Example.** A buyer needs 1000 MT. Supplier A can supply 800 MT, and supplier B offers 200 MT of
the same grade. The deal becomes one buyer with two supplier legs, not a lost 1000 MT order. The
same agent plans freight: how many containers of which size, whether to stuff at one port, and
whether a small leg goes as LCL. It uses quotes from Cogoport / Freightos / Maersk instead of a
fixed 25 MT per container.

**Order of work.** 13.4 (a deal with several supplier legs) has to exist before 13.3 can approve a
composition with two suppliers. 13.1 and 13.2 can start first behind `DEAL_COMPOSITION_ENABLED`,
with compositions limited to one supplier until 13.4 lands. The decisions on firm-offer wording,
lot mixing and the Shipping Bill come before the first two-supplier trade.

### 14 Starting the company (non-technical: registrations, accounts, set-up)

Everything a person must do outside the code to run an Indian export-import company legally. The
software cannot issue a PI or PO without most of it (exporter IEC / GSTIN / LUT / AD code on every
document), and escrow and payout providers will not open accounts without the company documents and
a website. Items already listed elsewhere are repeated here with "also n.n" so this area can be used
as the one start-up checklist.

**Order to do it in** (each step needs the one before):
1. Decide the legal form and the registered office (decisions).
2. Directors' DSC → incorporation (CIN, company PAN and TAN).
3. AD Category-I bank current account + EEFC account.
4. GST registration → IEC → ICEGATE + AD code at the ports → LUT for the year.
5. RCMC for each product (Spices Board, APEDA, FIEO) and Udyam; FSSAI / plant quarantine as the products need.
6. Domain, e-mail and website (needed for KYB) → escrow / payout provider accounts → QIMA, Maersk, forwarder, CHA, insurer.
7. CA, compliance calendar and accounting from the first day of trading.

Fees, forms and deadlines change; confirm each step with the CA before filing. The product examples
(spices, basmati) follow the HS codes used in testing; the real list comes from the catalogue.

### 21 Apify: more about each buyer and supplier

**Search quality (2026-11-17).** A review of the cached searches showed the product name sent to Apify was
sometimes the official HS description ("Vegetables; onions, whole, cut … importers, AE") or a vague
heading ("spices" for turmeric), and that lead-list sites, promotion bodies and foreign exporters came
back as buyers. Those are fixed (21.1). Results for a good name are mostly real companies (spice houses in
Hamburg, Spanish seafood wholesalers, Gulf rice traders).

**More companies.** Today one source (a web search, about 5 companies per line) supplies every buyer and
supplier. 21.3 adds list actors, best first: Indian exporter registries (Spices Board / APEDA / MPEDA:
registered exporters with a number to verify), Indian B2B listings with prices, importer association
member lists, names extracted from the "top importers" articles the search drops today, business
directories with activity codes, and Google Places listings for wholesale hubs; then buy requests,
food tenders and the clients of competing-origin exporters.

**More information.** The biggest gains, in order: searching in the market's language (no actor cost),
contact persons and the buyer's own product range (extensions of the contact finder the scan already
runs), trade-fair exhibitor lists (a new, higher-quality source), then company age / reputation in the
screening actor.

### 20 Buyers & Suppliers as a trading book

**Why.** The directory listed what a scan found (check, fit, last scan outcome) but not what happened with
each company: whether PortLess wrote to it, whether it replied, what it asked for or quoted, whether it
traded. The outreach introduction promises "reply 'not interested' and we will not write again", yet a
second prospect wrote again; exporter "buyers" stayed in the funnel; names stayed as scanned.

**Where it stands.** Each company now shows its relationship stage, activity, prices and a score; the
do-not-contact list is kept by replies and staff and honoured by outreach in every prospect. Open: the
legal check on cold e-mail per market, a company page with people and notes, supplier performance from
real deals, duplicates, bulk actions.

### 19 Prospect desk — trading the prospect stage

**Why.** A prospect is a bet that one of its buyers and one of its suppliers will trade at a margin.
The trader's questions are: what is it worth, whose move is it and for how long, what price do I push
each side to, which quotes are going stale, and is it still worth the effort. Before 2026-11-17 the
Prospects page showed only funnel counts; the match board scored pairs but gave no walk-away prices, hid
expired quotes, used a different freight than the radar, and the outreach e-mail opened with the contact
finder's note to the operator.

**Where it stands.** The desk answers those questions per prospect and ranks the prospects; the board
shows walk-away / target prices and quote age. Open: outbound mail is failing on live data (P0), and the
moves the desk suggests (best and final, reconfirm, price discovery, park / drop) still need their
e-mails and buttons.

### 18 How opportunities are found

**Today.** Top-down only: product × market pairs (catalogue, scan dialog or defaults) → UN Comtrade demand
and price gap → **gate** (no demand data or no measured gap = no opportunity) → web search for buyers and
suppliers of that pair → pair scores → expected profit. The buyer / supplier directory is a by-product,
never a source: on 2026-11-17 only 55 of 287 buyers were linked to an opportunity; 126 were dropped by the
Comtrade gate and 53 worldwide-search companies were never tied to a market.

**The idea.** Find opportunities from both ends: keep the market-led scan, and add opportunities that start
from companies (directory, enquiries, suppliers' offers, the worldwide search). Every opportunity, whatever
its source, is judged by the same expected profit and ready-to-start checklist (16), with its weaker price
basis shown — so more opportunities do not mean less honest ones.

**Order of work.** 18.1 company-led opportunities and the profit gate first (data already in the
directory, no extra Apify runs); then demand signals and supplier-led; the worldwide-search markets and
the role clean-up alongside; area 17 (fresher national statistics) makes the market-led half stronger.

### 17 Trade data beyond UN Comtrade

**Why.** Comtrade already covers every country, so more "Comtrade-like" actors are not the point. Its
gaps are: annual figures one to two years old, 6-digit codes that mix grades, some countries reporting
late (UAE: 2023) or without quantities (several UK lines), and India's export price only as a world
average. National statistics fix exactly these: UK HMRC, Eurostat Comext and US Census have free APIs
(plain connectors, no Apify cost); India's TradeStat has no API, so it is the one new Apify actor. On
2026-11-17, 126 of 287 directory buyers were dropped for "no live demand data" or "no measured price
gap" — the gaps these sources close.

**Order of work.** US Census key (no code) → UK HMRC (fixes the UK quantities) → India TradeStat actor
(fresh India-to-market prices for every market, and the UAE) → Eurostat Comext → mirror rule → the
merge rule and 8-digit mapping (with 15.1).

### 16 Opportunity Radar: profit and next step

**Where it stands (2026-11-17, after area 16).** The radar ranks by expected profit with a range,
each price with its basis, and knows before "Start prospect" whether the deal can proceed (reachable
buyers / suppliers, export policy, news, destination needs, a running deal). Its numbers travel into
the deal and are compared with the realised margin once deals settle. What is left is data, not code:
the mandi key (owner track A), freight quote keys (2.6), RoDTEP / drawback rates and eligibility (CA),
HS export policy confirmations, container and destination requirements per catalogue product (Ops),
and per-buyer import frequency (12, decision).

### 15 Exact product requirement per buyer and supplier

**The gap.** The directory knows that a company buys or sells an HS code ("onion"), not what it
buys or sells. Two onion buyers can want different goods: one 55 mm+ red onion in 25 kg mesh bags
by reefer every month, the other dehydrated flakes. Pairing, outreach and pricing treat them the
same today, and the exact requirement is only learned late, inside one prospect, and is lost to the
directory when that prospect closes.

**The idea.** Each catalogue product has a short specification template. Each buyer and supplier
record carries, per product, what that company itself has said about it, with the source and date
of every fact, and "not known" for the rest. Outreach and RFQs ask for exactly the unknown facts;
matching compares specifications; the agreed specification then travels unchanged into the PI, PO,
packing, inspection and cost sheet.

**Order of work.** 15.1 (template, then the two record shapes) first; 15.2 "from replies, and kept"
and "staff edit" give data at no extra scan cost; the website reader costs Apify runs and comes
after. 15.3 matching should only become a hard filter once enough records carry a specification,
otherwise every pair is filtered out as "not known"; until then an unknown attribute lowers the
score and is listed as still needed. Area 10 "product tree ids, packing standards, AQL and quality
specs per product line" is the Ops half of the template.
